   "text": "I read this as a reserpine-type skeleton and assigned each stereocentre from the wedges and hashes in the drawing.\n\n**Skeleton**\n- A 6-methoxyindole (the OMe sits meta to the carbon bearing the NH-side ring fusion) is fused to a tetrahydro-\u03b2-carboline.\n- The nitrogen of that ring system is also part of the quinolizidine ring.\n- The cyclohexane ring carries a CO2Me, an OMe and a 3,4,5-trimethoxybenzoyloxy group.\n\n**Stereocentres**\nI took each centre's neighbours in my SMILES order and compared the clockwise or anticlockwise sense of the drawing against the SMILES chirality mark.\n\n| Centre (drawing) | Drawn feature | SMILES mark |\n|---|---|---|\n| C\u2013OMe | OMe on hashed bond | @ |\n| C\u2013O-ester | O-ester on wedge | @@ |\n| Ring-fusion CH next to CH2\u2013N | hashed H | @@ |\n| N\u2013CH (next to indole) | wedged H | @ |\n| Ring-fusion CH next to CH2 | hashed H | @@ |\n| C\u2013CO2Me | ester on wedge | @@ |\n\nTwo of these (the ring-fusion CH next to CH2\u2013N and the N\u2013CH) differ from the reserpine SMILES I recalled from memory. I therefore kept the values derived from the drawing rather than the recalled ones.\n\nSMILES: CO[C@H]1[C@@H](C[C@@H]2CN3CCc4c([nH]c5cc(OC)ccc45)[C@H]3C[C@@H]2[C@@H]1C(=O)OC)OC(=O)c1cc(OC)c(OC)c(OC)c1"
